    <!-- Article 1 -->
    <item>
        <title>Why AR Outperforms VR in Neuromuscular Rehabilitation</title>
        <link>https://yourdomain.com/news/ar-vs-vr-rehabilitation</link>
        <pubDate>Mon, 12 Mar 2024 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
        <dc:creator><![CDATA[admin]]></dc:creator>
        <content:encoded><![CDATA[<p>Recent clinical studies show that Augmented Reality (AR) provides critical visual proprioceptive feedback that VR often obscures. By overlaying digital cues onto the real world, patients maintain spatial awareness while receiving precise stimulation feedback.</p><p>This article explores the neurological basis for AR's superiority in motor rehabilitation contexts, reviewing data from 12 partner clinics across North America and Europe.</p><h2>Key Findings</h2><ul><li>AR patients showed 23% faster motor recovery in upper-limb protocols</li><li>Reduced disorientation symptoms compared to VR cohort</li><li>Higher patient compliance rates (94% vs 78% for VR)</li></ul>]]></content:encoded>
        <excerpt:encoded><![CDATA[Recent clinical studies show that Augmented Reality (AR) provides critical visual proprioceptive feedback that VR often obscures, leading to better rehabilitation outcomes.]]></excerpt:encoded>
        <wp:post_id>100</wp:post_id>
        <wp:post_date><![CDATA[2024-03-12 00:00:00]]></wp:post_date>
        <wp:post_name><![CDATA[ar-vs-vr-rehabilitation]]></wp:post_name>
        <wp:status><![CDATA[publish]]></wp:status>
        <wp:post_parent>0</wp:post_parent>
        <wp:menu_order>0</wp:menu_order>
        <wp:post_type><![CDATA[post]]></wp:post_type>
